WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.859
Checkout needs a payment gateway to charge a card.

2
00:00:05.859 --> 00:00:09.839
The simple way is to build one right there: new AdyenGateway().

3
00:00:10.033 --> 00:00:14.208
Now a test needs a fake gateway, and a new tenant uses Stripe.

4
00:00:14.208 --> 00:00:15.892
Which code has to change?

5
00:00:17.467 --> 00:00:18.544
Checkout itself.

6
00:00:18.544 --> 00:00:22.045
It named the class, so the choice is welded into it.

7
00:00:22.267 --> 00:00:23.709
So take the choice out.

8
00:00:23.709 --> 00:00:27.033
Checkout asks for a gateway, and never names a class.

9
00:00:27.033 --> 00:00:29.604
Something else decides which one it gets.

10
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:31.716
That something is a factory.

11
00:00:31.716 --> 00:00:34.454
In production it builds an AdyenGateway.

12
00:00:34.454 --> 00:00:36.781
In a test it builds a FakeGateway.

13
00:00:36.781 --> 00:00:38.424
Checkout doesn't change.

14
00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:44.862
The Design Patterns book calls this Factory Method: a subclass overrides one create method.

15
00:00:44.862 --> 00:00:48.234
Since Java 8, a plain function does the same job.

16
00:00:48.234 --> 00:00:53.532
A Supplier in Java, a function type in Kotlin, often a constructor reference.

17
00:00:54.767 --> 00:00:56.741
Now some products must match.

18
00:00:56.741 --> 00:01:00.417
A card network gives you an authorizer and a refunder.

19
00:01:00.417 --> 00:01:04.707
A Visa auth code means nothing to Mastercard's refund endpoint.

20
00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:13.051
If every call site picks its own pair, one day a path that runs once a month takes a Visa authorizer and a Mastercard refunder.

21
00:01:13.051 --> 00:01:17.031
Its Visa auth code goes to Mastercard, where it means nothing.

22
00:01:17.233 --> 00:01:20.238
Abstract Factory puts that choice in one place.

23
00:01:20.238 --> 00:01:25.609
One interface makes every product of the family, with one implementation per family.

24
00:01:25.833 --> 00:01:30.039
The call site asks for the family once, and takes both products from it.

25
00:01:30.039 --> 00:01:33.719
It can't mix families, because it never picks a product itself.

26
00:01:33.933 --> 00:01:35.500
The cost runs one way.

27
00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:38.348
A new family is one more implementation.

28
00:01:38.348 --> 00:01:43.404
A new product, say a Tokenizer, changes the interface and every family.

29
00:01:44.633 --> 00:01:48.922
In code, the family is one interface with one method per product.

30
00:01:48.922 --> 00:01:51.495
A caller gets it from one factory call.

31
00:01:52.667 --> 00:01:53.232
The rule.

32
00:01:53.232 --> 00:01:57.066
The caller asks for what it needs, and never names the class.

33
00:01:57.066 --> 00:02:00.836
When products must match, one choice picks the whole family.

34
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:01.763
Quick check.

35
00:02:01.763 --> 00:02:04.242
Card ops adds AMEX to the network enum.

36
00:02:04.242 --> 00:02:07.548
Where does the build fail, and which callers change?

37
00:02:09.933 --> 00:02:16.592
It fails in the factory's switch, because it has no default branch, so you add an Amex branch there.

38
00:02:16.592 --> 00:02:17.790
No caller changes.

39
00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:19.788
Callers only ask for a family.
